= State machine =

== Overview ==
A '''state machine''' is a model of behavior in which a system occupies a state and changes state according to transitions triggered by inputs, events, conditions, or actions.

State machines are used in software, hardware, protocols, control systems, user interfaces, formal verification, and theoretical computation.

== State ==
A state is an abstract representation of information relevant to future behavior.

It is not necessarily a physical location.

Two physical configurations can be treated as the same state when they have the same relevant future behavior.

== Transitions ==
A transition maps a current state and possibly an input or condition to a next state.

A deterministic model has at most one specified next state for each relevant condition.

Nondeterministic models can allow several.

== Finite-state machines ==
A finite-state machine has finitely many states.

Finite-state machines are important in hardware control, protocol design, parsers, regular-language recognition, and workflow modeling.

But:

<pre>
state machine ≠ finite-state machine by definition
</pre>

The state space can be infinite.

== Outputs ==
Moore machines associate outputs with states.

Mealy machines associate outputs with transitions.

Other state-machine formalisms add hierarchy, concurrency, timing, probabilities, or continuous dynamics.

== Relation to automata ==
[[Automaton (computation)|Automata]] are formal state-transition machines studied for computational behavior and language recognition.

“State machine” is often used more broadly in engineering and software modeling.

